  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'PAPER_EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code generation and execution agents has opened new frontiers in software development, automated problem-solving, and intelligent programming assistance. However, the growing capability of these autonomous code agents also introduces significant security risks, including vulnerability exploitation, malicious code injection, and unsafe execution behaviors. This paper surveys the current landscape of code agents, examining their architectures, capabilities, and applications. We present a comprehensive review of red-teaming methodologies specifically designed for evaluating the security and robustness of code agents. We discuss the emerging field of automated red-teaming for code agents, including recent frameworks such as RedCodeAgent [1], and analyze their effectiveness in identifying security vulnerabilities. Furthermore, we draw connections to foundational work in deep learning architectures that enable these advanced agents, including the Transformer architecture [2] and residual learning frameworks [3]. We conclude with a discussion of open challenges and future research directions in code agent security evaluation.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a significant paradigm shift in how software is developed, tested, and maintained. Unlike traditional code generation tools, code agents are autonomous systems capable of writing, executing, debugging, and iterating on code in response to complex tasks and natural language instructions. These agents have gained widespread adoption in domains ranging from software engineering [1] to scientific computing and data analysis.\n\nThe primary value proposition of code agents lies in their ability to understand natural language requirements, translate them into executable code, execute the code in controlled environments, analyze the output, and iteratively refine their approach. This capability is particularly powerful when agents are equipped with code interpreters, allowing for dynamic execution, debugging, and interactive programming workflows.\n\nHowever, this increased autonomy and capability introduces substantial security concerns. Code agents operate in environments where they can write and execute arbitrary code, making them potential attack vectors if not properly secured. Malicious actors could potentially exploit vulnerabilities in code agents to:\n\n1. Execute unauthorized code on target systems\n2. Access sensitive data through code generation\n3. Create backdoors or exploit vulnerabilities in target applications\n4. Bypass safety constraints designed to prevent harmful code execution\n\nThese risks necessitate robust security evaluation frameworks specifically designed for code agents. Traditional security testing methodologies may not adequately address the unique challenges posed by autonomous agents that can generate and execute arbitrary code in response to input prompts.\n\nThis paper provides a comprehensive survey of the current state of code agents and the emerging field of red-teaming for these systems. We organize our discussion as follows: Section 2 reviews related work in code agents and security evaluation. Section 3 discusses code agent architectures and capabilities. Section 4 examines red-teaming methodologies for code agents. Section 5 presents challenges and future directions. Section 6 concludes the paper.\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\nThe field of code agents and their security evaluation sits at the intersection of several research areas, including large language models, code generation, software security, and adversarial testing.\n\n### 2.1 Foundational Architectures\n\nThe development of modern code agents relies heavily on advances in deep learning architectures. The Transformer architecture, introduced in [2], has become the backbone of most state-of-the-art language models. This architecture, which replaced recurrent and convolutional approaches with self-attention mechanisms, enabled models to process and generate text with unprecedented quality and efficiency. The Transformer's ability to capture long-range dependencies and parallelize computation has been instrumental in developing models capable of understanding and generating complex code structures.\n\nThe success of deep neural networks in various domains has been partly enabled by innovations in network architecture design. The introduction of residual learning frameworks, as detailed in [3], addressed the degradation problem in very deep networks, enabling the training of networks with hundreds or even thousands of layers. This advancement has been crucial for developing the large-scale models that power modern code agents.\n\n### 2.2 Code Agent Systems\n\nThe field of code-generating agents has seen rapid development, with several prominent systems emerging in recent years. These systems typically combine large language models with code execution environments, tools for file system manipulation, and iterative refinement capabilities. The RedCodeAgent framework [1] represents a specialized effort in this domain, focusing specifically on the security evaluation of code agents.\n\nRedCodeAgent introduces an automated red-teaming approach for evaluating diverse code agents against security vulnerabilities. The framework is designed to systematically probe code agents with adversarial prompts aimed at eliciting insecure code generation, vulnerability exploitation, or other security-relevant behaviors. This work builds upon the observation that while code agents offer significant productivity benefits, their security properties have not been thoroughly studied.\n\nThe RedCodeAgent paper [1] identifies several categories of security vulnerabilities specific to code agents, including:\n- Unsafe code execution\n- Prompt injection attacks\n- Data leakage through code output\n- Manipulation of execution environments\n- Bypassing of safety constraints\n\n### 2.3 Security Evaluation and Red-teaming\n\nTraditional security evaluation methodologies, such as penetration testing and vulnerability scanning, have been adapted for machine learning systems. However, the unique characteristics of code agents—autonomy, code execution capability, and iterative refinement—necessitate novel evaluation approaches.\n\nAutomated red-teaming frameworks, including those specifically designed for code agents [1], aim to systematically generate adversarial inputs that test the boundaries of agent capabilities and safety mechanisms. These frameworks often employ techniques such as:\n\n1. **Prompt crafting**: Generating adversarial prompts designed to elicit specific vulnerable behaviors\n2. **Iterative refinement**: Using agent outputs to inform subsequent test cases\n3. **Coverage analysis**: Ensuring comprehensive testing across different agent capabilities and configurations\n4. **Vulnerability classification**: Categorizing discovered vulnerabilities by type and severity\n\nThe work in [1] demonstrates that existing code agents exhibit significant security vulnerabilities, underscoring the need for continued research in this area.\n\n## 3. Code Agent Architectures and Capabilities\n\nModern code agents typically employ a multi-component architecture that integrates several key capabilities:\n\n### 3.1 Language Model Foundation\n\nAt the core of most code agents lies a large language model, often fine-tuned on code-specific datasets. These models, built upon Transformer architectures [2], are capable of understanding natural language instructions and generating corresponding code in various programming languages. The self-attention mechanisms within the Transformer enable these models to capture complex relationships between code elements and instructions.\n\n### 3.2 Code Execution Environment\n\nCode agents typically operate within sandboxed execution environments that allow them to run generated code and observe results. These environments may include:\n- Python interpreters for code execution\n- File system access for reading and writing files\n- Web browsing capabilities\n- Database query interfaces\n\nThe execution environment is critical for enabling agents to verify their code produces correct results and to iterate on solutions. However, it also represents a potential attack surface that must be carefully secured.\n\n### 3.3 Tool Integration\n\nAdvanced code agents integrate with various tools and APIs, enabling them to:\n- Search for information online\n- Query databases\n- Access external APIs\n- Manipulate files and directories\n- Communicate with other systems\n\nThis tool integration significantly expands agent capabilities but also introduces additional security considerations.\n\n### 3.4 Iterative Refinement\n\nCode agents typically employ iterative refinement processes where they:\n1. Generate initial code based on user instructions\n2. Execute the code and observe results\n3. Analyze errors or unexpected behavior\n4. Revise the code based on observations\n5. Repeat until the task is completed or a maximum number of iterations is reached\n\nThis iterative process enables agents to solve complex problems but also requires careful monitoring to prevent malicious behavior from emerging through repeated interactions.\n\n## 4. Red-teaming Methodologies for Code Agents\n\nRed-teaming for code agents involves systematically testing these systems to identify security vulnerabilities and evaluate safety mechanisms. The RedCodeAgent framework [1] represents a significant advancement in this area, providing an automated approach to red-teaming diverse code agents.\n\n### 4.1 RedCodeAgent Framework\n\nThe RedCodeAgent system [1] introduces several novel contributions to automated red-teaming for code agents:\n\n**Automatic Prompt Generation**: The framework automatically generates adversarial prompts designed to elicit vulnerable behaviors from code agents. These prompts are crafted to test various attack vectors, including prompt injection, unsafe code generation, and environment manipulation.\n\n**Vulnerability Taxonomy**: RedCodeAgent employs a comprehensive taxonomy of vulnerabilities specific to code agents, enabling systematic classification and analysis of discovered security issues.\n\n**Coverage-based Testing**: The framework ensures comprehensive testing across different code agent capabilities, including code generation, code interpretation, tool usage, and multi-step reasoning.\n\n**Evaluation Metrics**: RedCodeAgent provides quantitative metrics for measuring the security posture of different code agents, enabling comparative analysis across systems.\n\n### 4.2 Attack Categories\n\nBased on the RedCodeAgent taxonomy [1], code agent vulnerabilities can be categorized into several groups:\n\n**Direct Vulnerabilities**:\n- Unsafe code generation (e.g., generating code that executes system commands)\n- SQL injection through code generation\n- File system manipulation (e.g., accessing unauthorized files)\n- Network requests to malicious endpoints\n\n**Indirect Vulnerabilities**:\n- Prompt injection leading to unsafe behavior\n- Data leakage through code output\n- Manipulation of execution environments\n- Bypassing of content filters\n\n**Emerging Vulnerabilities**:\n- Multi-turn attack chains\n- Cross-agent coordination attacks\n- Resource exhaustion attacks\n\n### 4.3 Testing Methodologies\n\nEffective red-teaming for code agents requires systematic testing methodologies:\n\n1. **Static Analysis**: Examining agent configurations and capabilities without active interaction\n2. **Dynamic Testing**: Engaging the agent with adversarial prompts and analyzing outputs\n3. **Coverage Analysis**: Ensuring all agent capabilities are tested\n4. **Statistical Analysis**: Using statistical methods to evaluate vulnerability frequencies across multiple test runs\n\n### 4.4 Evaluation Challenges\n\nRed-teaming code agents presents several unique challenges:\n- **Non-determinism**: Code agents may produce different outputs for the same input\n- **Context dependency**: Vulnerabilities may only manifest in specific context windows\n- **Temporal aspects**: Some vulnerabilities require extended interaction sequences\n- **Evaluator bias**: The choice of test cases may influence discovered vulnerabilities\n\n## 5. Challenges and Future Directions\n\nDespite significant progress in code agent security evaluation, several challenges remain:\n\n### 5.1 Evaluation Standardization\n\nThe field lacks standardized evaluation benchmarks for code agent security. Work such as RedCodeAgent [1] represents important steps toward standardization, but broader community adoption is needed.\n\n### 5.2 Automated Defense Mechanisms\n\nWhile automated red-teaming frameworks [1] can identify vulnerabilities, automated defense mechanisms that can protect against discovered attack patterns are still in early stages of development.\n\n### 5.3 Adversarial Robustness\n\nCode agents must be robust against sophisticated adversarial attacks. Research is needed on:\n- Robust prompt engineering techniques\n- Formal verification methods for agent behavior\n- Runtime monitoring and intervention capabilities\n\n### 5.4 Multi-Agent Security\n\nAs multi-agent systems become more common, security considerations must extend beyond individual agent security to inter-agent communication and coordination security.\n\n### 5.5 Ethical Considerations\n\nRed-teaming activities raise important ethical questions about:\n- Responsible disclosure of vulnerabilities\n- Balance between security testing and potential misuse\n- Access control for security evaluation tools\n\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nThe field of code agents has advanced rapidly, enabling unprecedented capabilities in automated code generation and execution. However, these capabilities also introduce significant security challenges that must be addressed through rigorous evaluation and robust security mechanisms.\n\nThis paper has surveyed the current landscape of code agents and red-teaming methodologies, highlighting the importance of frameworks like RedCodeAgent [1] in systematically evaluating agent security. We have discussed the foundational architectures that enable code agents [2, 3], examined the unique vulnerabilities specific to these systems, and outlined challenges for future research.\n\nKey takeaways include:\n1. Code agents introduce novel security vulnerabilities that require specialized evaluation frameworks\n2. Automated red-teaming, as demonstrated by RedCodeAgent [1], provides a systematic approach to vulnerability discovery\n3. The field would benefit from standardized evaluation benchmarks and shared vulnerability taxonomies\n4. Continued research is needed on both offensive (red-teaming) and defensive mechanisms\n\nAs code agents become more prevalent in software development and other domains, rigorous security evaluation must remain a top priority. The work of researchers in areas like RedCodeAgent [1] provides valuable tools and methodologies for ensuring these powerful systems can be deployed safely and responsibly.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang, et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[2] Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar, et al. \"Attention Is All You Need.\" arXiv:1706.03762, 2017.\n\n[3] Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, et al. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" arXiv:1512.03385, 2015.\nPAPER_EOF"
